.TH QQ 1 "2026-09-22" "qq 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
qq \- ask an AI model a quick question
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qq
.RB [ \-hclrvwxy ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR model ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR model ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR text ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR secs ]
.RB [ \-L
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I prompt ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qq
sends
.I prompt
to an AI model and prints the answer on standard output as plain text.
The request tells the model to answer briefly and directly, without markdown.
.PP
It works with any OpenAI-compatible
.B /chat/completions
endpoint.
Endpoints are set up as gateways, each listing its models, in a JSON file; see
.BR CONFIGURATION .
.PP
When standard input is not a terminal,
.B qq
reads it (up to 1 MiB) and appends it to the prompt inside
.B <stdin>
tags.
If no prompt words are given, the input alone is the prompt.
Option parsing stops at the first word that is not an option, so
.B "qq how do I use ls \-la"
keeps
.B \-la
in the prompt.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-l
List the configured models, one per line, in the order they appear in the
file, and exit. Each line is
.IB gateway / model
followed by the tools, MCP servers and
.B allow_danger
that model ends up with once its gateway's settings and its own are combined.
The model that would be used is marked with
.RB \(lq "* " \(rq,
which is the one named by
.B \-p
when it is given, and the default otherwise.
API keys are not checked, so a model whose key is not set is still listed.
.TP
.B \-c
Add a line describing the environment (operating system, shell, current
directory) to the system prompt.
.TP
.B \-r
Let the model read files; see
.BR "LOCAL TOOLS" .
.TP
.B \-w
Let the model create and edit files.
.TP
.B \-x
Let the model run shell commands.
.TP
.BI \-p " model"
Use
.I model
for this call instead of the default.
.I model
is
.IB gateway / model \fR,
or just a model name when only one gateway has a model of that name; if
several do, the call is refused and the
.IB gateway / model
spellings that would settle it are listed.
A gateway name alone selects that gateway's
.BR default_model .
A
.RB \(lq / \(rq
separates a gateway only when what precedes it is a gateway's name, so model
names such as
.B meta/llama\-3
work bare.
.TP
.BI \-d " model"
Save
.I model
as the default in the configuration file, written as
.IB gateway / model \fR,
then run the prompt if one was given.
The file is replaced atomically; a symbolic link to it keeps working and the
file mode is kept.
.TP
.BI \-s " text"
Append
.I text
to the system prompt.
.TP
.BI \-t " secs"
Time limit for the whole call, tool rounds included.
Time spent answering approval prompts does not count.
The default is the configuration's
.BR timeout ,
or 120 seconds if it has none.
.TP
.B \-y
Answer every approval with yes.
Writes, edits, commands and reads outside the working directory are carried out
as soon as the model asks, and no terminal is needed, so
.B qq
will do them in a script or a cron job where it would otherwise refuse for want
of one.
The flag is not enough by itself: the model's gateway must have
.BR "allow_danger" : true ,
which is off unless set, and
.B \-y
on any other model is an error (exit 2) that does nothing.
Each action is still printed on standard error before it happens.
MCP calls are unaffected, never having been approved: they run on the server,
not on this machine.
.TP
.BI \-L " file"
Append a debug log of the whole exchange to
.IR file ,
which is created if needed; a file that cannot be opened is an error (exit 2)
and nothing is sent.
Each event is one line, beginning with a local timestamp to the millisecond and
its UTC offset:
.B command
(the whole command line as invoked, quoted the way a shell would need it),
.BR start ,
.BR model ,
.BR timeout ,
.B request
(the URL and the entire request body, chat and MCP alike),
.B response
(status, elapsed time and the whole body),
.BR request\-failed ,
.BR tool\-round ,
.B tool\-call
(name and the arguments as the model sent them),
.B approval
(the answer and how long it took),
.BR tool\-result ,
.B tool\-repeat
(a call answered from history instead of done again),
.BR mcp\-call ,
.BR mcp\-result ,
.BR answer ,
.B failed
and
.BR exit .
Entries are flushed as they happen, so the log still explains an interrupted
run, and control characters in bodies and results are escaped, so an event
cannot span lines or steer the terminal the log is read on.
The log holds the prompts, any file the model read and any command output
verbatim, so treat it as being as private as the conversation; the API key is
not in it, travelling in a header that is not logged.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options; everything after it is the prompt.
.SH LOCAL TOOLS
.BR \-r ,
.B \-w
and
.B \-x
may be combined in any order (for example
.B \-rw
or
.BR \-xw ),
and each of them implies
.BR \-c .
.B qq
defines the tools and runs them itself:
.TP
.B \-r
.BR read_file " (at most 256 KB), " list_directory ", and " search_files
(plain-text search of the files under a directory, at most 200 matches,
skipping hidden and binary files).
.TP
.B \-w
.B write_file
and
.B edit_file
(replaces text that occurs exactly once in the file).
.TP
.B \-x
.B run_command
runs a command with
.BR "/bin/sh \-c"
in the current directory, keeping 32 KB of each output stream, and stops it at
the time limit.
.PP
Reads inside the current directory run immediately; a read outside it,
including through a symbolic link, is confirmed first.
Every write, edit and command is confirmed on the terminal
.RI ( /dev/tty ),
which shows exactly what will happen; only
.B y
or
.B yes
approves.
Without a terminal the action is refused, as is a prompt left unanswered for
120 seconds.
Refusals are reported to the model as the tool's result, and hold for the rest
of the run: an identical call is refused again without asking.
Calls that were carried out are remembered the same way, so a model that
repeats itself cannot write, read or run anything twice, nor be asked about it
twice; a round containing only repeats ends the run with an error.
MCP calls are remembered alongside local ones, so an identical query is
answered from the earlier result rather than put to the server again.
A write, edit or command that runs discards the remembered reads, so a read
that follows a change is carried out again.
Each tool that runs is logged on standard error.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file holds a JSON object:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  "default": "litellm/nemotron",
  "system_prompt": "Answer concisely in plain text.",
  "timeout": 120,
  "gateways": {
    "litellm": {
      "endpoint": "http://localhost:4000",
      "api_key_env": "LITELLM_API_KEY",
      "default_model": "nemotron",
      "tools": ["read", "write"],
      "mcp_servers": ["searxng_mcp", "files_mcp"],
      "models": {
        "nemotron": {
          "id": "nemotron-3.5-lightning",
          "mcp_servers": ["searxng_mcp"]
        },
        "readonly": { "id": "nemotron-3.5-lightning", "tools": ["read"] }
      }
    }
  }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
The top-level keys are
.BR default
(a
.IB gateway / model \fR),
.BR system_prompt ,
.BR timeout ,
and
.BR gateways ,
which is required.
The old
.B profiles
key is no longer read.
.PP
A gateway is one endpoint.
It requires
.B endpoint
and
.BR models ,
an object with at least one model in it.
It also accepts
.BR default_model ,
.BR api_key_env ,
the name of an environment variable that holds a bearer token \(em the
variable itself, not the key, and it must be set to a non-empty value when a
model on the gateway is used or
.B qq
exits 2 \(em and defaults for its models:
.BR temperature ,
.BR max_tokens ,
.BR timeout
and
.BR system_prompt .
.PP
A gateway also sets the ceiling for what its models may do.
.B tools
lists which of
.BR read ,
.B write
and
.B exec
its models may be used with (\c
.BR \-r ,
.B \-w
and
.BR \-x ),
.B mcp_servers
names the MCP servers they may offer, and
.BR "allow_danger" : true
is what
.B \-y
requires.
A gateway grants only what it lists.
.PP
A model is a member of
.BR models ,
keyed by the name typed after
.BR \-p .
Every member is optional.
.B id
is the name sent to the API and defaults to the key.
.BR temperature ,
.BR max_tokens ,
.B timeout
and
.B system_prompt
override the gateway's.
.BR tools
and
.B mcp_servers
may list a subset of the gateway's; leaving one out inherits all of them and
.B []
means none.
.B allow_danger
may only be
.BR false .
Listing anything the gateway does not grant is a configuration error, not
something ignored.
.PP
The whole file is checked on every run, so an error in a gateway that is not
being used is still reported.
Unknown or retired keys (including
.BR mcp ,
.B backend
and a boolean
.BR tools ),
repeated names, a gateway with no models, and a
.B default_model
that is not one of its models are errors too.
.PP
.B tools
is a ceiling, not a switch: the tool is offered to the model only when the
matching flag is given, and a flag the model's
.B tools
lack is refused with an error (exit 2) rather than silently ignored.
Local tools and MCP servers are independent: a model with
.B "mcp_servers" : []
can still use
.BR \-r .
.PP
With
.BR mcp_servers ,
the named LiteLLM MCP servers are offered to the model as tools, with
.B require_approval
set to
.BR always ,
so the gateway returns every tool call instead of running any itself.
.B qq
runs each one through the proxy's
.BR /mcp\-rest/tools/call .
With
.B never
the gateway also tried to run
.BR qq 's
own local tools, which it cannot, and told the model they had failed when they
had not.
MCP and local tool calls together are limited to 20 rounds.
.PP
The system prompt is the built-in instruction, then the top-level
.BR system_prompt ,
the gateway's, the model's, and
.BR \-s ,
skipping any that are empty.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/qq/config.json
Default location of the configuration file.
.TP
.I config.example.json
An example configuration, distributed with
.BR qq .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B QQ_CONFIG
Path of the configuration file, overriding the default location.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
If set to an absolute path, the configuration file is
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/qq/config.json .
.TP
.B SHELL
Reported by
.BR \-c .
.PP
API keys are read from the environment variable named by a gateway's
.BR api_key_env .
They are never stored in the configuration file. A gateway that names a
variable which is unset or empty is a configuration error when one of its
models is used; omit
.B api_key_env
for an endpoint that needs no key.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The endpoint or the network failed, or the model gave an empty answer.
.TP
.B 2
Usage or configuration error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
qq what is the capital of france
git diff | qq summarize this change
qq \-c how do I list files by size
qq \-r what does the debug target in the Makefile do
qq \-rw add a clean target to the Makefile
qq \-p litellm/nemotron \-s "Reply in French." say hello
qq \-d litellm/nemotron
qq \-l
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
The
.I README.md
file distributed with
.BR qq .
